  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1101 AM MDT Fri Jun 19 2026\n\nLooks like we are entering a more static weather pattern in the \nmid/long term as the mid and upper highs become stacked over \nnorthern Mexico and the Baja. In the short term for today we \ncontinue with the mid level high over southern New Mexico. Most of \nthe deeper layered moisture that was over the CWA Thursday has \nvanished in place this morning. Dewpoints are already lowering, with \n30s west mixing out as far east as the RG Valley this afternoon. \nBest moisture will remain over Otero/Hudspeth Counties, with \nafternoon dewpoints still in the 40s to 50 and PWs around one inch. \nThus isolated afternoon thunderstorms look plausible for the Sacs \nand Hudspeth Co, with a few over Hudspeth lasting into early evening.\n\nAforementioned static pattern will turn up the heat knob after \ntoday. While today will see near 100 El Paso metro area and lower \nvalley, high temps Saturday through much of next will gradually \nwarm, with Tue-Fri looking to be the hottest. NBM MOS temps for this \nperiod run from 103-107 across the lowlands. 90th percentile highs \nfor the same period range from 105-110. Needless to say we are \nprobably looking at several days of Heat Advisories and could \napproach Heat Warnings as we enter the two week window of hottest\ntemperatures climatologically.",
